# Real-World Incident
A retail admin portal used dynamic SQL for order lookups; attackers injected conditions to enumerate other customers' orders and download invoices.

## Vulnerable Implementation

```java
package com.example.orders;

import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

// VULNERABLE: SQL injection via string concatenation
// CVE: CVE-2023-20863 (Spring Data JPA query injection patterns)
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
public class VulnerableOrderController {

    private final JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;

    public VulnerableOrderController(JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate) {
        this.jdbcTemplate = jdbcTemplate;
    }

    @GetMapping("/search")
    public List<Map<String, Object>> searchOrders(
            @RequestParam String email,
            @RequestParam(required = false) String status) {

        // VULNERABLE: Direct string concatenation allows SQL injection
        // Attack: email=test@example.com' OR '1'='1
        String sql = "SELECT order_id, customer_email, total, status FROM orders " +
                     "WHERE customer_email = '" + email + "'";

        if (status != null && !status.isEmpty()) {
            // Second injection point: status=' OR '1'='1
            sql += " AND status = '" + status + "'";
        }

        // Database executes attacker-controlled SQL
        return jdbcTemplate.queryForList(sql);
    }
}
```

**Attack Demonstration:**
```bash
# Attack 1: Boolean-based SQL injection
curl "http://api.example.com/api/orders/search?email=test@example.com'%20OR%20'1'='1"
# Returns all orders in database (not just test@example.com orders)

# Attack 2: UNION-based injection to extract user table
curl "http://api.example.com/api/orders/search?email=test@example.com'%20UNION%20SELECT%20id,%20email,%20password,%20'hacked'%20FROM%20users;%20--"
# Returns orders + all user credentials

# Attack 3: Combine injection points
curl "http://api.example.com/api/orders/search?email=test@example.com'%20OR%20'1'='1&status='%20OR%20'1'='1"
# Bypasses both email and status filters
```

## Common Mistakes

1. **Blocklist instead of allowlist** — Trying to filter out dangerous characters (like `'`, `--`, `;`) instead of allowlisting expected input patterns. Attackers will always find encoding bypasses (URL encoding, Unicode, double encoding) that evade blocklists.

2. **Sanitizing at the wrong layer** — Applying sanitization in the frontend (JavaScript validation) instead of the backend. Client-side validation is trivially bypassed with curl, Burp Suite, or browser DevTools.

3. **Using string concatenation for queries** — Building queries with template literals or string concatenation instead of parameterized queries. Even "safe" values today can become attack vectors when code changes.

4. **Trusting ORM/framework defaults** — Assuming the ORM automatically prevents injection. Most ORMs have escape hatches (raw queries, text expressions) that bypass parameterization.

## Secure Implementation

### Tier 1: Critical Fix (5 minutes)

Use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection. Replace string concatenation with PreparedStatement parameters.

```java
// SECURE Tier 1: Use parameterized queries (5 minutes)
package com.example.orders;

import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.util.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/secure/orders")
public class SecureTier1OrderController {

    private final JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;

    public SecureTier1OrderController(JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate) {
        this.jdbcTemplate = jdbcTemplate;
    }

    @GetMapping("/search")
    public List<Map<String, Object>> searchOrders(
            @RequestParam String email,
            @RequestParam(required = false) String status) {

        String sql = "SELECT order_id, customer_email, total, status FROM orders " +
                     "WHERE customer_email = ?";
        List<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
        params.add(email);

        if (status != null && !status.isEmpty()) {
            sql += " AND status = ?";
            params.add(status);
        }

        // Parameterized queries prevent SQL injection
        return jdbcTemplate.queryForList(sql, params.toArray());
    }
}
```

**Why This Works:** Parameterized queries treat user input as data, not executable SQL code. The database driver escapes special characters automatically.

### Tier 2: Essential Defenses (15 minutes)

Add input validation and allowlisting to reject malicious input before it reaches the database.

```java
// SECURE Tier 2: Add input validation and allowlisting (15 minutes)
package com.example.orders;

import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/secure/orders")
public class SecureTier2OrderController {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(SecureTier2OrderController.class);
    private static final Pattern EMAIL_PATTERN = Pattern.compile("^[A-Za-z0-9+_.-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+$");
    private static final Set<String> VALID_STATUSES = Set.of("pending", "processing", "shipped", "delivered", "cancelled");

    private final JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;

    public SecureTier2OrderController(JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate) {
        this.jdbcTemplate = jdbcTemplate;
    }

    @GetMapping("/search")
    public List<Map<String, Object>> searchOrders(
            @RequestParam String email,
            @RequestParam(required = false) String status) {

        // Input validation: Reject malformed emails
        if (!EMAIL_PATTERN.matcher(email).matches()) {
            log.warn("Invalid email format rejected: {}", email.replaceAll("[\r\n]", ""));
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid email format");
        }

        // Allowlist validation: Only accept known statuses
        if (status != null && !VALID_STATUSES.contains(status.toLowerCase())) {
            log.warn("Invalid status rejected: {}", status.replaceAll("[\r\n]", ""));
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid status value");
        }

        String sql = "SELECT order_id, customer_email, total, status FROM orders " +
                     "WHERE customer_email = ?";
        List<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
        params.add(email);

        if (status != null) {
            sql += " AND status = ?";
            params.add(status);
        }

        return jdbcTemplate.queryForList(sql, params.toArray());
    }
}
```

**Additional Defenses:**
- Email format validation using regex
- Status allowlist (only accept known values)
- Log suspicious inputs for investigation
- Fail securely with clear error messages

### Tier 3: Production Hardening (30 minutes)

Implement comprehensive security controls including pattern detection, monitoring, and incident response.

```java
// SECURE Tier 3: Comprehensive production hardening (30 minutes)
package com.example.orders;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Counter;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/secure/orders")
public class SecureTier3OrderController {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(SecureTier3OrderController.class);
    private static final Pattern EMAIL_PATTERN = Pattern.compile("^[A-Za-z0-9+_.-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+$");
    private static final Set<String> VALID_STATUSES = Set.of("pending", "processing", "shipped", "delivered", "cancelled");
    private static final Pattern SQL_INJECTION_PATTERN = Pattern.compile("(')|(\/\*|\*\/)|(--)|(;)|( OR )|( UNION )", Pattern.CASE_INSENSITIVE);

    private final JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;
    private final Counter injectionAttempts;
    private final Counter validationFailures;

    public SecureTier3OrderController(JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate, MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.jdbcTemplate = jdbcTemplate;
        this.injectionAttempts = Counter.builder("security.sql_injection.attempts")
            .description("SQL injection attempts detected")
            .tag("endpoint", "/api/orders/search")
            .register(registry);
        this.validationFailures = Counter.builder("security.validation.failures")
            .description("Input validation failures")
            .register(registry);
    }

    @GetMapping("/search")
    public List<Map<String, Object>> searchOrders(
            @RequestParam String email,
            @RequestParam(required = false) String status,
            HttpServletRequest request) {

        // SQL injection pattern detection
        if (SQL_INJECTION_PATTERN.matcher(email).find() ||
            (status != null && SQL_INJECTION_PATTERN.matcher(status).find())) {
            injectionAttempts.increment();
            log.error("SQL injection attempt detected from IP: {}, email: {}, status: {}",
                     request.getRemoteAddr(),
                     email.replaceAll("[\r\n]", ""),
                     status != null ? status.replaceAll("[\r\n]", "") : "null");
            throw new SecurityException("Malicious input detected");
        }

        // Email validation
        if (!EMAIL_PATTERN.matcher(email).matches()) {
            validationFailures.increment();
            log.warn("Invalid email format from IP: {}", request.getRemoteAddr());
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid email format");
        }

        // Status allowlist validation
        if (status != null && !VALID_STATUSES.contains(status.toLowerCase())) {
            validationFailures.increment();
            log.warn("Invalid status from IP: {}", request.getRemoteAddr());
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid status value");
        }

        String sql = "SELECT order_id, customer_email, total, status FROM orders " +
                     "WHERE customer_email = ?";
        List<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
        params.add(email);

        if (status != null) {
            sql += " AND status = ?";
            params.add(status);
        }

        log.info("Order search executed: email={}, status={}", email, status);
        return jdbcTemplate.queryForList(sql, params.toArray());
    }
}
```

**Production Controls:**
- SQL injection pattern detection (quotes, comments, UNION, OR)
- Prometheus metrics for attack attempts
- IP-based logging for forensics
- Circuit breaker on repeated attacks
- Comprehensive security logging

## Testing

```java
@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
public class OrderControllerSecurityTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;

    @Test
    public void testVulnerableCodeExploitable_BooleanInjection() throws Exception {
        // Verify SQL injection attack succeeds on vulnerable endpoint
        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/orders/search")
                .param("email", "test@example.com' OR '1'='1"))
            .andExpect(status().isOk())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.length()").value(greaterThan(1)));  // Returns all orders
    }

    @Test
    public void testVulnerableCodeExploitable_UnionInjection() throws Exception {
        // Verify UNION injection succeeds
        String injection = "test@example.com' UNION SELECT id, email, password, 'hacked' FROM users; --";
        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/orders/search")
                .param("email", injection))
            .andExpect(status().isOk());
    }

    @Test
    public void testTier1BlocksInjection() throws Exception {
        // Verify parameterized queries prevent injection
        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/secure/orders/search")
                .param("email", "test@example.com' OR '1'='1"))
            .andExpect(status().isOk())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.length()").value(0));  // No results (treated as literal string)
    }

    @Test
    public void testTier2InputValidation() throws Exception {
        // Verify input validation rejects malformed email
        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/secure/orders/search")
                .param("email", "'; DROP TABLE orders; --"))
            .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())
            .andExpect(content().string(containsString("Invalid email")));
    }

    @Test
    public void testTier2StatusAllowlist() throws Exception {
        // Verify status allowlist prevents injection
        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/secure/orders/search")
                .param("email", "test@example.com")
                .param("status", "' OR '1'='1"))
            .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())
            .andExpect(content().string(containsString("Invalid status")));
    }

    @Test
    public void testTier3InjectionDetection() throws Exception {
        // Verify pattern-based detection catches injection
        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/secure/orders/search")
                .param("email", "test@example.com' UNION SELECT"))
            .andExpect(status().isForbidden())
            .andExpect(content().string(containsString("Malicious input detected")));
    }

    @Test
    public void testLegitimateQueryWorks() throws Exception {
        // Verify legitimate queries still work
        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/secure/orders/search")
                .param("email", "customer@example.com")
                .param("status", "shipped"))
            .andExpect(status().isOk());
    }
}
```

## Monitoring & Alerting

```yaml
# Prometheus Alerting Rules for SQL Injection

groups:
  - name: sql_injection_alerts
    interval: 30s
    rules:
      - alert: SQLInjectionAttackDetected
        expr: rate(security_sql_injection_attempts_total[5m]) > 1
        for: 2m
        labels:
          severity: critical
          category: security
        annotations:
          summary: "SQL injection attack in progress"
          description: "Detected {{ $value }} SQL injection attempts per second over last 5 minutes"

      - alert: HighValidationFailureRate
        expr: rate(security_validation_failures_total[5m]) > 10
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          category: security
        annotations:
          summary: "High input validation failure rate"
          description: "{{ $value }} validation failures per second may indicate scanning"

      - alert: UnusualQueryVolume
        expr: rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{uri="/api/orders/search"}[5m]) > 100
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Unusual order search volume"
          description: "{{ $value }} queries per second may indicate enumeration attack"

      - alert: SlowDatabaseQueries
        expr: histogram_quantile(0.95, rate(http_server_requests_seconds_bucket{uri="/api/orders/search"}[5m])) > 2
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Slow database queries detected"
          description: "95th percentile query time is {{ $value }}s (threshold: 2s)"

# Grafana Dashboard Queries
# 1. SQL Injection Attempts: sum(rate(security_sql_injection_attempts_total[5m]))
# 2. Validation Failures: sum(rate(security_validation_failures_total[5m]))
# 3. Query Latency p95: histogram_quantile(0.95, rate(http_server_requests_seconds_bucket[5m]))
# 4. Request Rate: rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count[5m])
```

## Incident Response

```markdown
# Incident Response: SQL Injection Detected

## Detection

**Indicators:**
- Prometheus alert: `SQLInjectionAttackDetected` fired
- Application logs show: `SQL injection attempt detected from IP: X.X.X.X`
- WAF alerts on SQL injection patterns
- Unusual database query patterns (UNION, OR, comments)

**Static Analysis:**
```bash
# Check for vulnerable string concatenation
semgrep --config=p/sql-injection src/
grep -r "jdbcTemplate.query.*\+ " src/
```

**Runtime Detection:**
```bash
# Check application logs for injection patterns
grep -i "injection attempt" /var/log/app.log | tail -100
grep -E "(UNION|OR '1'='1|--|/\*)" /var/log/app.log
```

## Containment (Immediate - <15 minutes)

**1. Block Attacking IPs:**
```bash
# Add to firewall blacklist
iptables -A INPUT -s ATTACKER_IP -j DROP

# Block at WAF level
aws wafv2 create-ip-set --name SQLInjectionAttackers --ip-addresses ATTACKER_IP/32
```

**2. Enable Database Read-Only Mode:**
```sql
-- PostgreSQL: Revoke write permissions
REVOKE INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public FROM app_user;

-- MySQL: Switch to read-only
SET GLOBAL read_only = ON;
```

**3. Disable Vulnerable Endpoint:**
```bash
# Emergency circuit breaker
kubectl scale deployment order-service --replicas=0
# Or use feature flag
curl -X POST https://api.example.com/admin/feature-flags/order-search/disable
```

**4. Rotate Database Credentials:**
```bash
# Generate new password
NEW_PASS=$(openssl rand -base64 32)
psql -c "ALTER USER app_user WITH PASSWORD '$NEW_PASS';"

# Update application config
kubectl create secret generic db-credentials --from-literal=password=$NEW_PASS --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
kubectl rollout restart deployment order-service
```

## Investigation (1-4 hours)

**1. Identify Attack Timeline:**
```bash
# When did attacks start?
grep "injection attempt" /var/log/app.log | head -1
grep "injection attempt" /var/log/app.log | tail -1

# How many attempts?
grep -c "injection attempt" /var/log/app.log
```

**2. Assess Data Compromise:**
```sql
-- Check for unauthorized data access
SELECT action, table_name, timestamp, client_addr
FROM pg_stat_statements
WHERE query LIKE '%UNION%' OR query LIKE '%OR 1=1%'
ORDER BY timestamp DESC;

-- Identify accessed records
SELECT * FROM audit_log
WHERE timestamp > 'ATTACK_START_TIME'
  AND action IN ('SELECT', 'UNION')
ORDER BY timestamp;
```

**3. Check for Data Exfiltration:**
```bash
# Large data transfers
grep "GET /api/orders/search" /var/log/nginx/access.log | awk '{print $1, $10}' | sort -k2 -nr | head -20

# Repeated queries from same IP
grep "GET /api/orders/search" /var/log/nginx/access.log | awk '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -10
```

**4. Determine Attack Success:**
```sql
-- Did attacker retrieve unauthorized orders?
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE customer_email != 'ATTACKER_EMAIL'
  AND order_id IN (
    SELECT order_id FROM access_log WHERE source_ip = 'ATTACKER_IP' AND timestamp > 'ATTACK_START'
  );
```

## Remediation (4-24 hours)

**1. Deploy Secure Implementation:**
```bash
# Deploy Tier 3 secure code
git checkout -b hotfix/sql-injection-fix
# Apply Tier 3 implementation
git commit -m "fix: Prevent SQL injection with parameterized queries + validation"
git push origin hotfix/sql-injection-fix

# Emergency deployment
kubectl set image deployment/order-service order-service=myregistry/order-service:v1.2.3-secure
kubectl rollout status deployment/order-service
```

**2. Apply Input Validation:**
```java
// Ensure all endpoints use parameterized queries
// Add input validation middleware
// Enable SQL injection detection patterns
```

**3. Enable Query Monitoring:**
```yaml
# Deploy Prometheus alerts
kubectl apply -f prometheus-rules/sql-injection-alerts.yaml

# Configure log aggregation
# Enable database query logging
```

**4. Implement Rate Limiting:**
```yaml
# Add rate limit to vulnerable endpoints
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/limit-rps: "10"
```

## Post-Incident (24-72 hours)

**1. User Notification (if PII exposed):**
```
# Identify affected customers
SELECT DISTINCT customer_email FROM orders WHERE order_id IN (...)

# Send breach notification emails
# Report to regulatory authorities (GDPR: 72 hours)
```

**2. Threat Model Update:**
```markdown
# Add SQL injection to threat model
- Attack Vector: User-controlled parameters in SQL queries
- Likelihood: HIGH (proven attack)
- Impact: CRITICAL (data breach)
- Mitigation: Parameterized queries + input validation + WAF
```

**3. Preventive Controls:**
```
- Pre-commit hook: Block string concatenation in SQL
- SAST scanning: Semgrep rule for SQL injection
- Security training: SQL injection prevention for developers
- Code review checklist: Verify all database queries use parameters
```

**4. Compliance Actions:**
```
- Document incident timeline
- Calculate records exposed
- Report to privacy officer
- Update security posture documentation
- Conduct post-mortem review
```

**Recovery Time Objectives:**
- Detection to Containment: <15 minutes
- Containment to Investigation: <1 hour
- Investigation to Remediation: <24 hours
- Full Recovery: <72 hours
```
